          "type": "text",
          "text": "\"When converting an Ethereum ECDSA signature to JSON, the `v` component was being emitted as a plain integer value (e.g., `27`) instead of the hex‑encoded quantity format used by the rest of the library (e.g., `\\\"0x1b\\\"`). This inconsistency broke compatibility with tools that expect the `v` field to follow the same encoding rules as `r` and `s`, leading to parsing errors and incorrect signature handling in downstream workflows. The required behavior is that any serialization of a signature – whether as a JSON object with named fields or as a three‑element tuple – must represent `v` using the library’s quantity type, which renders as a hex string prefixed with `0x`. Additionally, deserialization must be able to read this hex quantity back into the internal representation, preserving the correct parity logic for both EIP‑155 and legacy signatures. The fix should ensure that when a signature is serialized, the `v` field appears as a hex string like `\\\"0x1b\\\"`, and that round‑tripping through JSON preserves the original signature values without loss or type mismatch. The implementation must handle both the map‑based representation (with keys `v` or `yParity`) and the tuple representation used by certain encodings, applying the same quantity conversion in each case.\"\n",
